    check are in technical.md.j2. Build: python build_case_study.py -#}
{%- set pt = n.policy_table -%}
{%- set best = pt["model_best"] -%}
{%- set capp = pt["model_capacity"] -%}
{%- set capg = pt["model_capacity_promo_rule"] -%}
{%- set bau = pt["bau_rule"] -%}
{%- set baucap = pt["bau_within_limits"] -%}
{%- set orc = pt["oracle (true best arm)"] -%}
{%- set pb = n.paired["model_best - bau_rule"] -%}
{%- set pbc = n.paired["model_best - model_capacity"] -%}
{%- set pcl = n.paired["model_capacity - bau_within_limits"] -%}
{%- set pgl = n.paired["model_capacity_promo_rule - bau_within_limits"] -%}
{%- set pgr = n.paired["model_capacity_promo_rule - model_capacity"] -%}
{%- set N = n.n_outlets -%}
{%- set NAME = {"0": "No action", "1": "App message", "2": "Price promo", "3": "Display kit", "4": "Rep visit"} -%}
{%- set mde3 = tval("pilot_power", "mde_80_power", months=3, analysis="adjusted") -%}
{%- set side = tval("pilot_power", "outlets_per_side", months=3, analysis="raw") -%}
{%- set se = tval("pilot_power", "se_difference", months=3, analysis="adjusted") -%}
{%- set margin = -30000 -%}
{%- set pw_g = phi(pgl.true_difference / se - 1.96) -%}
{%- set pw_c = phi(pcl.true_difference / se - 1.96) -%}
{%- set sw_g = phi((pgl.true_difference - margin - 1.96 * se) / se) -%}
{%- set sw_0 = phi((0 - margin - 1.96 * se) / se) -%}
{%- set qd = n.qini_evidence | selectattr("arm", "equalto", 3) | first -%}
{%- set qr = n.qini_evidence | selectattr("arm", "equalto", 4) | first -%}
{%- set kit_be = n.cost["3"] / n.value_per_outcome -%}
{%- macro spend(p) -%}{{ ((pt[p]["%_zalo"] * n.cost["1"] + pt[p]["%_promo"] * n.cost["2"] + pt[p]["%_display"] * n.cost["3"] + pt[p]["%_rep"] * n.cost["4"]) / 100) | round(-3) }}{%- endmacro -%}
## 1. The decision on one page

{{ box_begin("The question") }}

Each month the trade team gives each of {{ N | vnd }} beer outlets one action: no action, an app message, a
price promo, a display kit or a sales rep visit. Display kits and rep visits are limited. **Can a model that
predicts the effect of each action for each outlet choose better than today's rule (business as usual, BAU), and how should we test
it?**

{{ box_end() }}

**What we found** on {{ n.n_test_outlets | vnd }} outlets the model never saw. Money is extra net margin per outlet
per month, in VND (gross margin of the extra cases minus the cost of the action).

* **In the simulation, the model chooses the right action twice as often as BAU** (both without limits). It picks the truly best
  action for {{ n.agreement["all rows"] | f(0) }}% of outlet-months, against {{ n.agreement_bau["all rows"] | f(0) }}%
  for BAU. This can be checked only because the data is simulated. What the data itself shows is that the model
  ranks outlets for display kits and rep visits better than chance.
* **The limits on display kits and rep visits are expensive.** Without them the model would earn
  about {{ pbc.est_difference | r3 | vnd }} VND more per outlet-month than within them (about
  {{ (best.est_gain - capg.est_gain) | r3 | vnd }} more than with the promo rule), about {{ (pbc.est_difference * N) | mvnd }} million VND
  per month across all outlets. This result is clear. It counts the cost of each extra kit and visit, not fixed
  costs such as more reps or a larger kit stock. It assumes kits for about {{ best["%_display"] | f(0) }}% and visits for about
  {{ best["%_rep"] | f(0) }}% of outlet-months (6% and 8% today); less capacity earns less.
* **Within today's limits, the data cannot show a gain over BAU yet.** The simulation knows that the model with
  the promo rule earns {{ pgl.true_difference | vnd }} VND more per outlet-month than BAU held to the same limits.
  A real project would not know this. It would see only the estimate, {{ pgl.est_difference | vnd }} VND (range
  {{ pgl.ci_low | vnd }} to {{ pgl.ci_high | vnd }}), which is close to zero.
* **One known weakness: price promos.** The model believes a promo pays everywhere. The data suggests it
  does not pay where our brand already has a large share (above about 60%) of the outlet's beer volume. A simple rule should reduce the
  damage: no promo where our brand share is above 60%.

**What we recommend.**

1. Run a randomised pilot for 3 months. 10% of outlets get a random action. The other outlets are split at random
   into two halves of about {{ side | vnd }}: one half follows the model within the limits, with the promo rule;
   the other follows BAU within the same limits. The model half needs about {{ ((spend("model_capacity_promo_rule") | float - spend("bau_within_limits") | float) * side) | mvnd }} million VND
   more action budget per month than BAU (about {{ ((spend("model_capacity_promo_rule") | float - spend("bau_within_limits") | float) * side * 3) | mvnd }} million over the 3 months), mostly for
   promos. The estimated gains are already net of this spending, but the budget needs approval.
2. Expect a useful but not certain answer. If the simulated gain is real, the pilot shows a clear difference
   only about {{ (100 * pw_g) | round | int }} times in 100. The decision therefore uses a switch rule: switch to the
   model if its estimated gain is positive and the low end of its range is above a margin set in advance by the
   business (for example {{ margin | vnd }} VND per outlet-month). If the simulated gain is
   real, this switches about {{ (10 * sw_g) | round | int }} times in 10; if the model is no better than BAU, it still
   switches about {{ (10 * sw_0) | round | int }} times in 10. Real effects may be weaker. Stop early only if the
   model is far worse than BAU at the checks after months 1 and 2.
3. Build a business case for more rep capacity or display kits: the limits are the largest lever found.


## 2. The business problem

**Who decides what.** The trade marketing team decides once a month, for each outlet, which single action it
gets that month. Outlets are bars, restaurants, groceries, minimarts and modern trade stores (supermarket and
convenience chains) in five regions.

**The five actions** and what they cost per outlet per month (illustrative):

| Action | What it is | Cost (VND) | Limited? | Pays above |
|-------------|-------------------------------------|---------|--------|-----------|
{%- for k in ["0", "1", "2", "3", "4"] %}
| {{ NAME[k] }} | {{ n.arm_descriptions[k] }} | {{ n.cost[k] | vnd }} | {{ "yes" if k in n.capacity_full else "no" }} | {{ "" if k == "0" else ((n.cost[k] / n.value_per_outcome) | f(2 if n.cost[k] < n.value_per_outcome else 1)) ~ " cases" }} |
{%- endfor %}

Each extra case of beer ordered brings {{ n.value_per_outcome | vnd }} VND of gross margin. An action pays for
itself (its break-even) when it adds more cases than its cost divided by this margin: the last column. The limits
are set per region and month.

**Why the best action per outlet is not obvious.**

* Costs differ a lot, so the action with the largest effect is not always the one that earns most. A display kit
  adds about {{ n.naive.display_kit.true_average_effect | f(1) }} cases on average, below its break-even of
  {{ kit_be | f(1) }}: it pays only when it goes to the right outlets.
* The limits allow display kits for about 6% and rep visits for about 8% of a region's outlets each month.
* The same action works very differently by outlet: a rep visit pays in a busy bar with falling sales and does
  little in a small grocery with steady sales; a display kit pays in a grocery with coolers.
* Some actions lose money: a promo where our brand already dominates mostly discounts cases the outlet would have
  bought anyway.
* Past data is not a clean experiment: part of past actions followed the BAU rule, so simply comparing outlets
  that got an action with those that did not gives the wrong answer. In the data, outlets that got a display kit
  ordered *fewer* cases than others, although a display kit truly adds cases.

## 3. What we did

1. **Data.** {{ n.n_outlets | vnd }} outlets over {{ n.n_months }} months ({{ n.n_rows | vnd }} outlet-months).
   {{ n.randomised_share | pct(0) }} of the actions came from a randomised trade test, the rest from the BAU rule.
2. **Model.** For every outlet-month the model estimates how many extra cases each action would bring, from the
   outlet's profile and recent orders. Net value = extra cases x {{ n.value_per_outcome | vnd }} VND minus the cost.
3. **Decision rule.** Give each outlet the action with the highest expected net value, no action if none pays.
   With the limits: each month and region, rep visits and display kits go to the outlets where they are worth most,
   up to the limit; the other outlets get a promo or a message only where it pays.
4. **Grading on unseen outlets.** The model was built on {{ n.n_train_outlets | vnd }} outlets and graded on
   {{ n.n_test_outlets | vnd }} other outlets. Each outlet-month received only one action, so nobody saw what the
   other actions would have done there. A second, separate model fills in these missing outcomes for the grading;
   because it is separate, the model does not grade its own predictions. Each result comes with a range (95%
   confidence interval).
5. **Checks against the truth.** Because the data is simulated, the true value of every decision is known. Each
   estimate is shown next to the true value, so the reader can see where the method is right and where it is not.

