  Without limits the estimate is positive ({{ pb.est_difference | vnd }}) but its range just includes zero. The pilot
  compares the actual orders of the two halves directly, so this shortfall does not carry over to it.
* *Failed check:* in bars and restaurants the model does worse than simply giving every outlet a rep visit
  ({{ tval("stability_channel", "gain_vs_best_single_arm", group="on_trade_bar") | r3 | vnd }} and
  {{ tval("stability_channel", "gain_vs_best_single_arm", group="on_trade_restaurant") | r3 | vnd }} VND per outlet-month, both ranges below
  zero), likely because it gives many of them promos: the promo weakness again.
* *Spending:* the model spends more on actions than BAU, not less: about {{ spend("model_best") | float | vnd }} VND per
  outlet-month against {{ spend("bau_rule") | float | vnd }} for BAU as written, and {{ spend("model_capacity_promo_rule") | float | vnd }}
  (model with the promo rule) against {{ spend("bau_within_limits") | float | vnd }} within the limits. Its gains are net of this extra spending.

**Where the actions go.** Without limits the model gives rep visits mostly to bars and restaurants and display
kits to groceries and minimarts, in line with the truth. Modern trade stores never get a display kit or a rep
visit: these were never tried there, so there is no evidence either way. Under the limits, the outlets that lose
their kit or visit mostly get a price promo: {{ capp["%_promo"] | f(0) }}% of all outlet-months get a promo under
the model within limits, {{ capg["%_promo"] | f(0) }}% with the promo rule, against {{ baucap["%_promo"] | f(0) }}%
under BAU held to the limits. This is where the promo weakness matters.

{{ figure("business_promo_by_share", width="84%", caption="The price promo weakness, by our brand's share of the outlet's beer volume. Orange: the model's predicted extra cases from a promo. Blue: what the data shows, with its 95% range. Above the dashed line (2 cases) the promo pays for its cost.", source=False) }}

{{ need(10) }}
**The main weakness.** Where our brand share is lowest, a promo adds up to about
{{ tval("promo_vs_share_observed", "actual", bin="[0.03, 0.29]") | f(0) }} cases; in the middle it adds about 1.5 to
5, with one band below break-even; above a share of about 60% the data suggests it does not pay. The model's line is much flatter and stays above
break-even everywhere, so it gives promos where they do not pay. Two likely causes: brand share is a rough survey
estimate, and the promo effect is small next to normal swings in orders. Until it is measured better, the rule "no promo above a share of 60%" should protect the
margin. The 60% cutoff was chosen with the simulation's truth in view and read from the outlets used for grading, so the
value of the rule shown here is illustrative; on the outlets used for building the model the picture is the same
but weaker: above 60% a promo adds about 1 to 2 cases, at or below its break-even. Outlets without a survey estimate are not affected by the rule (promo allowed). The order in which limited actions are filled (rep visits first, then display kits) was also
set with the truth in view; a check of the other order without the truth found no clear difference. In the simulation the
rule adds {{ pgr.true_difference | vnd }} VND per outlet-month; the data cannot confirm it yet (estimated
{{ pgr.est_difference | vnd }}, range {{ pgr.ci_low | vnd }} to {{ pgr.ci_high | vnd }}).

## 5. Recommendation: a pilot

**What to test.** Each month and region, give rep visits and display kits to the outlets where the model expects
the most value, up to the limits; give the remaining outlets a promo or a message only where it is expected to pay
and, for the promo, only where our brand share is 60% or below.

**How.**

1. *Random groups.* A fixed 10% of outlets, drawn at random once, get a random action each month, drawn among
   the actions still available: kits and visits only up to this group's share of the limits. The other outlets are split at random within
   each region into two halves of about {{ side | vnd }}: one follows the model, the other BAU held to the same
   limits. Each region's kits and visits are shared between the three groups in proportion to their size. The
   random 10% gives the data to grade and rebuild the next model (the two halves only compare the two ways of choosing).
   It earns less than the model would; that is the price of clean data.
2. *Length.* 3 months, analysed with the adjusted analysis (below). A longer pilot hardly helps.
3. *Check after months 1 and 2.* Stop early only if the model is far worse than BAU: at these early checks
   the 99.9% range (not the usual 95%) must lie entirely below zero, so that chance alone rarely stops a good
   policy.
4. *Decide at the end.* Switch to the model if the estimated difference is positive and the lower end of its range
   is above a margin chosen in advance by the business, for example {{ margin | vnd }} VND per outlet-month;
   otherwise keep BAU and keep collecting randomised data. The margin is a business decision: how much downside
   risk is acceptable for a way of choosing the simulation expects to be better. With {{ margin | vnd }}, the rule switches
   about {{ (10 * sw_g) | round | int }} times in 10 if the simulated gain is real, and about
   {{ (10 * sw_0) | round | int }} times in 10 if the model is in fact no better than BAU.
5. *Budget.* The model half spends about {{ (spend("model_capacity_promo_rule") | float - spend("bau_within_limits") | float) | r3 | vnd }} VND more per outlet-month on actions than BAU,
   about {{ ((spend("model_capacity_promo_rule") | float - spend("bau_within_limits") | float) * side * 3) | mvnd }} million VND over the 3 months, mostly on promos; this needs approval
   before the pilot starts.
6. *Record for every outlet-month* the prediction, the action given, the group, and the orders. Without this the
   next model cannot be graded.
7. *Rebuild the model every quarter*, or sooner when costs, limits or the outlet base change.

{{ need(11) }}
**How large a gain can the pilot detect?**

{{ table("pilot_power", cols=["months", "analysis", "mde_80_power"], rename={"months": "Months", "analysis": "Analysis", "mde_80_power": "Smallest difference"}, caption="Smallest true difference (VND per outlet-month) between the two halves that the pilot detects with 80% probability, by length and analysis. Raw: compares the two halves directly. Adjusted: compares outlets after accounting for their orders before the pilot.", source=False) }}

The gain the simulation expects within the limits ({{ pgl.true_difference | vnd }} VND) is below every number in
the table, because outlets differ so much from each other. At that gain a 3-month pilot with the adjusted analysis
shows a clear difference only about {{ (100 * pw_g) | round | int }} times in 100 (about
{{ (100 * pw_c) | round | int }} in 100 without the promo rule); a "no clear difference" result is therefore quite
possible even if the model is better. The pilot is still worth running: the stop rule limits the risk of a large loss, the switch
rule above does not require a clear difference, and it adds fresh randomised data under the new policy, which
today's data lacks. The adjusted analysis halves the smallest detectable difference, so it should be the main
analysis.

**A larger lever: capacity.** The limits cost about {{ pbc.est_difference | r3 | vnd }} VND per outlet-month under the
model without the promo rule (about {{ (best.est_gain - capg.est_gain) | r3 | vnd }} with it), about {{ (pbc.est_difference * N) | mvnd }} million VND per month across all outlets. This figure already
nets the per-action cost of each extra kit and visit, but not fixed costs such as hiring and training reps or a
larger kit stock and its logistics. It assumes about 3 times today's kits and 5 times today's visits; a smaller
increase earns less. It is worth a separate business case.

## 6. Risks and limitations

* **Simulated data.** The method was graded where the truth is known. Real effects may be weaker or noisier.
* **Promo effect.** The model's weakest point; partly limited for now by the brand share rule, whose value is not yet proven.
* **No evidence where an action was never tried.** Display kits and rep visits were never given to modern trade
  stores; the model does not use them there, and their effect there stays unknown until tested.
* **Simple limits.** One limit per region and month. Real rep capacity depends on routes and travel time.
* **One month at a time.** The analysis assumes an action affects only that month's orders. A display kit stays
  in the outlet longer.
* **Neighbouring outlets.** A promo in one outlet may take sales from the next one; this is not modelled.
* **Fixed promo cost.** A real promotion costs more where more is ordered, which makes promos in large, loyal
  outlets even less attractive.

## 7. Questions business readers ask

**Why not just compare outlets that got an action with outlets that did not?**
Because the BAU rule chose who got what: it gave display kits to outlets with coolers and rep visits to large
bars, which order differently anyway. The raw comparison says a display kit loses cases when it truly adds them.

**Why not run a simple A/B test for each action?**
An A/B test gives the average effect of an action. The decision here is per outlet: the same rep visit adds many
cases in a busy bar with falling sales and few in a small grocery with steady sales.

**Which outlets get a rep visit? A display kit?**
Rep visits: bars and restaurants that order often, especially when their sales are falling. Display kits:
groceries and minimarts with coolers.

**Why does the model give so many promos when the limits apply?**
Once the kits and visits are used up, the promo is the action with the highest predicted value left. The model
overrates it where our brand share is high; hence the brand share rule.

**Can we trust the estimated gains?**
They are measured on outlets the model never saw, by a separate model. With shuffled actions the gain falls to
at most about {{ n.placebo_null_max | r3 | vnd }} VND, against {{ n.placebo.actual_gain | r3 | vnd }} with the real ones. For the ways of
choosing in this report the true values fall inside the ranges, but the model's estimates within the limits are
{{ (capg.true_gain - capg.est_gain) | r3 | vnd }} to {{ (capp.true_gain - capp.est_gain) | r3 | vnd }} VND below the truth, and the technical report shows simpler rules (for example "rep visit for
all") that are badly misjudged. The ranges are wide, which is why the report separates what is shown from what is not.

**What does the pilot cost?**
All three groups share today's limits, so the pilot needs no extra kits or visits. It does need more action
budget: the model half spends about {{ (spend("model_capacity_promo_rule") | float - spend("bau_within_limits") | float) | r3 | vnd }} VND
more per outlet-month than BAU, mostly on promos ({{ capg["%_promo"] | f(0) }}% of its outlet-months against
{{ baucap["%_promo"] | f(0) }}%), about {{ ((spend("model_capacity_promo_rule") | float - spend("bau_within_limits") | float) * side) | mvnd }} million VND per month
for about {{ side | vnd }} outlets, about {{ ((spend("model_capacity_promo_rule") | float - spend("bau_within_limits") | float) * side * 3) | mvnd }} million VND over the 3 months. The estimated gains are already net of this spending, but the budget needs approval.
The random 10% earns less than the model would; this cost was not estimated. The other risk is a few months of a way of choosing that may be
no better than BAU; the stop rule catches only a large loss, so a moderate loss could last the full 3 months.

**Where are the details?**
In the technical report (same numbers, full method, every check) and the analysis code (a Jupyter notebook).

## 8. Words used in this report

The terms below are used with these meanings throughout the report.

| Word | Meaning |
|--------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| BAU | Business as usual: the current rule for choosing actions. |
| BAU as written | The BAU rule without the limits; it hands out more kits and visits than the limits allow. |
| BAU held to the limits | The BAU rule with its extra kits and visits removed at random down to the limits; the fair comparison for the model within the limits. |
| Outlet-month | One outlet in one month; each gets one action. |
| Gross margin | Margin on the extra cases: {{ n.value_per_outcome | vnd }} VND per case. |
| Net value | Gross margin of the extra cases minus the cost of the action. |
| Break-even | The number of extra cases at which an action exactly pays its cost. |
| Promo rule | No price promo where our brand share is above 60%; outlets without a share estimate are not affected. |
| Cost of the limits | How much more the model earns without the limits on kits and visits, net of their per-action cost but not of fixed costs (more reps, more kits in stock). |
| Range (95%) | The 95% confidence interval: the values the data is consistent with. "Includes 0" means the data cannot rule out no difference. |
| Clear | The whole range lies above zero (for a gain) or below zero (for a loss). |
| True value | Known only because the data is simulated; a real project only has the estimate. |
| Randomised pilot | A trial in which chance, not people, decides which outlets follow the model; the most reliable way to compare. |
| Adjusted analysis | Compares the two halves after accounting for each outlet's orders before the pilot; removes much of the noise. |
| 80% probability | If the true difference has this size, the pilot shows a clear result 8 times out of 10. |
| Shuffled-actions test | Refit the model after shuffling who got which action; a real model should then find nothing. |
| Brand share | Our brand's estimated share of the outlet's beer volume, from a survey. |
| Modern trade | Supermarket and convenience chains. |
